etd:question-19 a etd:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Who CAN I discriminate against?" ;
    etd:authoredBy etd:expert-10 ;
    etd:belongsToTheme etd:theme-10 ;
    etd:body """Okay, the title was inflammatory on purpose, but hear me out!\r
\r
What makes one characteristic ethically okay to consider during the application process (such as whether their education was relevant to the role) but one not (such as gender), besides the LAW? \r
\r
If we're going to be completely amoral and only focussed on metrics like staff retention, then women often take maternity leave for example, which causes issues with handovers and temporary staffing.  But it is obviously WRONG to discriminate against women for this.  Not just illegal but morally rubbish.\r
\r
Having a degree in horse psychotherapy is not relevant to a coding job, so not hiring someone for that reason is valid and it's not discrimination. \r
\r
These are extremes.  What about smokers (lower productivity and more time off on average) versus non-smokers?  Where is the line? What makes it DISCRIMINATION?\r
\r
LET'S GET PHILOSOPHICAL!""" ;
    etd:created_at "2025-07-23T10:40:30.401000+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    etd:expertId 10 ;
    etd:questionId 19 ;
    etd:themeId 10 ;
    etd:title "Who CAN I discriminate against?" ;
    etd:views 39 .

etd:question-21 a etd:Question ;
    rdfs:label "How do you feel about current efforts to regulate AI (like the EU AI Act)? Are they moving fast enough?" ;
    etd:authoredBy etd:expert-49 ;
    etd:belongsToTheme etd:theme-8 ;
    etd:body """Criteria\r
1) Adequate (do they address real risks?)\r
2) Timely (are they keeping pace with the speed of AI innovation?)\r
3) Effective (do they actually lead to safer, fairer AI in practice?)\r
\r
What we are looking for\r
A) The expert’s policy stance \r
B) Whether they think current laws are future-proof\r
C) Their views on global cooperation vs. fragmented governance\r
D) What they think is missing or urgently needed""" ;
    etd:created_at "2025-07-23T11:46:25.273000+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    etd:expertId 49 ;
    etd:questionId 21 ;
    etd:themeId 8 ;
    etd:title "How do you feel about current efforts to regulate AI (like the EU AI Act)? Are they moving fast enough?" ;
    etd:views 43 .

etd:theme-11 a etd:Theme ;
    rdfs:label "Education: Automated Essay Scoring Bias" ;
    etd:authoredBy etd:expert-5 ;
    etd:created_at "2025-07-21T00:07:26+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    etd:description """**Domain**: Education/Assessment  \r
**Description**: Automated Essay Scoring (AES) systems show systematic bias against students from marginalized groups. 2024 studies found GPT-4o incorrectly predicted academic failure for Black students 19% of the time vs. 12% for white students.\r
\r
**Ethical Challenges**:\r
\r
* **Linguistic Bias**: Against non-native English speakers and dialectal variations  \r
* **Cultural Bias**: Preference for mainstream cultural references and writing conventions  \r
* **Socioeconomic Bias**: Correlated with writing style differences across economic classes  \r
* **Educational Gap Perpetuation**: Biased algorithms can reinforce existing educational disparities\r
\r
**Public Datasets**:\r
\r
* **Primary**: ASAP (Automated Student Assessment Prize) Dataset  \r
* **URL**: https://www.kaggle.com/c/asap-aes/data  \r
* **Papers with Code**: https://paperswithcode.com/dataset/asap  \r
* **Source**: Kaggle Competition Data  \r
* **Content**: \\~12,800 essays from grades 7-10 across 8 different prompts  \r
* **Enhanced**: ASAP 2.0 Dataset (2024) \\- 24,000 argumentative essays with explicit bias considerations  \r
* **ASAP 2.0 URL**: https://the-learning-agency-lab.com/learning-exchange/asap-2-0-dataset/  \r
* **GitHub Implementation**: https://github.com/Turanga1/Automated-Essay-Scoring""" ;
    etd:domain_category "Education" ;
    etd:expertId 5 ;
    etd:model_category "Machine Learning" ;
    etd:name "Education: Automated Essay Scoring Bias" ;
    etd:problem_category "AI BIAS" ;
    etd:themeId 11 ;
    etd:views 0 .

etd:theme-13 a etd:Theme ;
    rdfs:label "Transportation: Rideshare Price Discrimination" ;
    etd:authoredBy etd:expert-5 ;
    etd:created_at "2025-07-21T00:13:33+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    etd:description """**Domain**: Transportation/Gig Economy  \r
**Description**: Pandey & Caliskan (2021) analyzed 100+ million rideshare samples in Chicago, finding that dynamic pricing algorithms systematically discriminate against neighborhoods with non-white and low-income populations.\r
\r
**Ethical Challenges**:\r
\r
* **Geographic Bias**: Neighborhoods with larger non-white populations charged significantly higher fares  \r
* **Socioeconomic Bias**: Higher poverty areas face pricing discrimination  \r
* **Algorithmic Opacity**: Dynamic pricing systems without transparency  \r
* **Historical Discrimination Reinforcement**: Perpetuation of historical transportation discrimination\r
\r
**Public Datasets**:\r
\r
* **Primary**: Chicago Transportation Network Provider (TNP) Trips Dataset  \r
* **URL**: https://data.cityofchicago.org/Transportation/Transportation-Network-Providers-Trips/m6dm-c72p  \r
* **Source**: City of Chicago Open Data Portal  \r
* **GitHub Analysis**: https://github.com/toddwschneider/chicago-taxi-data  \r
* **Dashboard**: https://toddwschneider.com/dashboards/chicago-taxi-ridehailing-data/  \r
* **Content**: 100+ million rideshare samples analyzed (2018-present)  \r
* **Includes**: Uber, Lyft, Via data with anonymized fare, time, and census tract location information""" ;
    etd:domain_category "Transport" ;
    etd:expertId 5 ;
    etd:model_category "Machine Learning" ;
    etd:name "Transportation: Rideshare Price Discrimination" ;
    etd:problem_category "AI BIAS" ;
    etd:themeId 13 ;
    etd:views 0 .

etd:theme-15 a etd:Theme ;
    rdfs:label "Insurance: Algorithmic Underwriting Bias" ;
    etd:authoredBy etd:expert-5 ;
    etd:created_at "2025-07-21T00:15:00+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    etd:description """**Domain**: Insurance/Risk Assessment  \r
**Description**: AI-powered insurance underwriting systems show bias in risk assessment and pricing, particularly affecting minority communities and certain geographic areas.\r
\r
**Ethical Challenges**:\r
\r
* **Proxy Discrimination**: Use of zip codes and other proxies that correlate with protected characteristics  \r
* **Historical Data Bias**: Training on historical data that reflects past discriminatory practices  \r
* **Disparate Impact**: Higher premiums for equivalent risk profiles in minority communities  \r
* **Regulatory Compliance**: Violation of fair lending and anti-discrimination laws\r
\r
**Public Datasets**:\r
\r
* **Communities and Crime Dataset**: UCI repository with socioeconomic and crime data  \r
* **URL**: https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/183/communities+and+crime  \r
* **German Credit Dataset**: Classic fairness benchmark for credit risk assessment  \r
* **URL**: https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/144/statlog+german+credit+data  \r
* **Default of Credit Card Clients**: Taiwan dataset for credit default prediction  \r
* **URL**: https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/350/default+of+credit+card+clients""" ;
    etd:domain_category "Finance" ;
    etd:expertId 5 ;
    etd:model_category "Machine Learning" ;
    etd:name "Insurance: Algorithmic Underwriting Bias" ;
    etd:problem_category "AI BIAS" ;
    etd:themeId 15 ;
    etd:views 0 .

etd:theme-16 a etd:Theme ;
    rdfs:label "Smart Cities: Urban Planning Algorithm Bias" ;
    etd:authoredBy etd:expert-5 ;
    etd:created_at "2025-07-21T00:15:37+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    etd:description """**Domain**: Urban Planning/Smart Cities  \r
**Description**: Smart city algorithms for resource allocation, traffic management, and urban planning show systematic bias against underserved communities.\r
\r
**Ethical Challenges**:\r
\r
* **Resource Allocation Bias**: Unequal distribution of city services and infrastructure investments  \r
* **Environmental Justice**: Disproportionate placement of polluting facilities in minority neighborhoods  \r
* **Digital Divide**: Algorithms that favor areas with better data coverage  \r
* **Participation Inequality**: Underrepresentation of marginalized voices in data collection\r
\r
**Public Datasets**:\r
\r
* **NYC Open Data**: Comprehensive city services and demographics data  \r
* **URL**: https://opendata.cityofnewyork.us/  \r
* **Boston Street Bump Dataset**: Crowdsourced road condition data showing geographic bias  \r
* **URL**: https://data.boston.gov/dataset/street-bump-pothole-reports  \r
* **311 Service Requests**: Multi-city datasets showing service request patterns  \r
* **NYC 311**: https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Social-Services/311-Service-Requests-from-2010-to-Present/erm2-nwe9  \r
* **Chicago 311**: https://data.cityofchicago.org/Service-Requests/311-Service-Requests/v6vf-nfxy""" ;
    etd:domain_category "Urban Planning" ;
    etd:expertId 5 ;
    etd:model_category "Machine Learning" ;
    etd:name "Smart Cities: Urban Planning Algorithm Bias" ;
    etd:problem_category "AI BIAS" ;
    etd:themeId 16 ;
    etd:views 0 .

etd:theme-17 a etd:Theme ;
    rdfs:label "E-commerce: Recommendation System Bias" ;
    etd:authoredBy etd:expert-5 ;
    etd:created_at "2025-07-21T00:16:16+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    etd:description """**Domain**: E-commerce/Recommendation Systems  \r
**Description**: Online marketplace recommendation algorithms show bias in product suggestions, affecting business opportunities for minority-owned businesses and perpetuating stereotypes.\r
\r
**Ethical Challenges**:\r
\r
* **Economic Opportunity Bias**: Reduced visibility for minority-owned businesses  \r
* **Stereotype Reinforcement**: Algorithms that reinforce gender and racial stereotypes in product recommendations  \r
* **Filter Bubbles**: Creation of echo chambers that limit exposure to diverse products and sellers  \r
* **Price Discrimination**: Dynamic pricing that varies based on user demographics\r
\r
**Public Datasets**:\r
\r
* **Amazon Product Review Dataset**: Large-scale product reviews with potential demographic indicators  \r
* **URL**: https://nijianmo.github.io/amazon/index.html  \r
* **Yelp Open Dataset**: Business reviews and ratings with geographic and demographic patterns  \r
* **URL**: https://www.yelp.com/dataset  \r
* **RecSys Challenge Datasets**: Annual competition datasets for recommendation systems  \r
* **URL**: https://recsys.acm.org/recsys-challenge/""" ;
    etd:domain_category "Marketing & Sales" ;
    etd:expertId 5 ;
    etd:model_category "Machine Learning" ;
    etd:name "E-commerce: Recommendation System Bias" ;
    etd:problem_category "AI BIAS" ;
    etd:themeId 17 ;
    etd:views 0 .

etd:theme-9 a etd:Theme ;
    rdfs:label "Financial Services: Mortgage Lending Discrimination" ;
    etd:authoredBy etd:expert-5 ;
    etd:created_at "2025-07-20T08:42:30+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    etd:description """**Domain**: Financial Services/Lending  \r
**Description**: Bartlett et al. analysis of HMDA dataset revealed systematic discrimination in mortgage lending, with Black and Latino borrowers paying higher interest rates despite similar risk profiles.\r
\r
**Ethical Challenges**:\r
\r
* **Racial/Ethnic Bias**: Black and Latino borrowers pay 7.9 and 3.6 basis points more respectively  \r
* **Economic Impact**: Results in $765M+ annually in excessive interest payments  \r
* **Digital Redlining**: Algorithmic discrimination in historically disadvantaged neighborhoods  \r
* **Intersectional Bias**: Particularly affects African American women\r
\r
**Public Datasets**:\r
\r
* **Primary**: Home Mortgage Disclosure Act (HMDA) Dataset  \r
* **URL**: https://www.consumerfinance.gov/data-research/hmda/  \r
* **Data Portal**: https://ffiec.cfpb.gov/data-publication/  \r
* **Historical Data**: https://catalog.data.gov/dataset/home-mortgage-disclosure-act-hmda-public-data-from-2007-2017  \r
* **Source**: Federal Financial Institutions Examination Council  \r
* **Content**: 88-90% of all U.S. mortgage loans since 1975, with enhanced data since 2017  \r
* **Includes**: Loan applications, approvals, denials, applicant demographics, loan terms, property location, lender information\r
\r
**Additional Dataset**:\r
\r
* **Supplementary**: Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac Loan-Level Dataset  \r
* **URL**: https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/thedevastator/2016-fannie-mae-and-freddie-mac-loan-level-datas""" ;
    etd:domain_category "Finance" ;
    etd:expertId 5 ;
    etd:model_category "Machine Learning" ;
    etd:name "Financial Services: Mortgage Lending Discrimination" ;
    etd:problem_category "AI BIAS" ;
    etd:themeId 9 ;
    etd:views 0 .

etd:answer-23 a etd:Answer ;
    rdfs:label "I would echo Holly & Adrian - really good points. Might I also suggest: Algorith" ;
    etd:answerId 23 ;
    etd:answersQuestion etd:question-16 ;
    etd:authoredBy etd:expert-47 ;
    etd:created_at "2025-07-22T17:38:51.958000+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    etd:description """I would echo Holly & Adrian - really good points. Might I also suggest:\r
\r
Algorithms can perpetuate or even amplify existing biases if they are trained on biased historical data (e.g., past job match/hiring decisions that favored certain genders, ethnicities, or age groups). Such bias can occur even if the relevant data on the CV is obfuscated from the model because other elements may 'proxy' this information. This is one of the major challenges to solve for a fair job matching AI.\r
\r
Additionally, CVs/Resumes usually contain at least some sensitive personal data. Improper handling can lead to data breaches or misuse. This must be mitigated somehow. Somewhat related is that we don't really know how ChatGPT will look to monetise, and for now OpenAI are being forced to retain ALL chatlogs due to a court decision (even before they would retain this unless you opted out) so who knows what may happen to this data if for example an organisation uses the OpenAI API for this process.""" ;
    etd:expertId 47 ;
    etd:hasVote etd:vote-74 ;
    etd:questionId 16 .

etd:answer-29 a etd:Answer ;
    rdfs:label "This is a very big and interesting idea to grapple with. In terms of **A**, I th" ;
    etd:answerId 29 ;
    etd:answersQuestion etd:question-20 ;
    etd:authoredBy etd:expert-5 ;
    etd:created_at "2025-07-23T17:20:52.196000+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    etd:description """This is a very big and interesting idea to grapple with. \r
\r
In terms of **A**, I think building a robust audit process starts with building a not-so robust audit process. I would say that there's a practical answer to this question and also a more 'philosphical' one in terms of how you do the practical process (ways of working).\r
\r
So the first stage of the practical process is creating your initial, relatively naive ethical AI approach:\r
\r
A good jumping off point is to begin conducting some research and using this to build an *ethical* framework for your audit process. First port of call is to select an ethical/policy basis that you believe in or you know is going to legally apply to your use case(s), e.g. AI for Good initiative recommendations. You can use this policy basis to codify the standards, transparency processes, & alignment with regulation you need to meet, and which you will need to test for adherence to in your *technical* audit process. Then you can build a simple technical auditing framework by answering the following questions: what statistical methods will be used to test data/model fairness/quality, and how will this vary based on the type of data/model? How do these tests relate back to the standards outlined in your ethical framework, and what is your acceptance threshold/definition of done? Again, you will most likely need to perform some kind of literature review here to select tools & algorithms that are the most robust in criteria that make sense to your team. You should discuss with technical & non-technical stakeholders as you do this to make sure as much is captured as possible. Crucially, these frameworks should be living artefacts that are regularly reviewed and reflected on. This should enable them to grow alongside changing science, policy, & your learning from auditing mistakes (there will be many of these, especially early on).\r
\r
This is the very basic level you need to reach before you can start making things more robust. Once you've got a simple approach down you can start to test out more layers & techniques, & based on their efficacy introduce them permanently into your frameworks. The sky is the limit here, but we have used the following approaches at my company to great success:\r
\r
Red teaming is a concept in cybersecurity wherein ethical hackers simulate cyberattacks against your organisation's systems. You can do the same thing for ethical AI; for example attempt to build an adversarial model that can infer protected characteristics from your model. Introducing documentation requirements is also helpful, because A it encourages people to think about things and B it makes it easier to decide on testing methods from your framework to apply. You can use Model Cards for model reporting and Datasheets/Spec Sheets for datasets to capture details like intended use, limitations, & testing conditions. Requiring audit trails during your audit & development processes can also make them more robust. Log all design decisions, training data sources, & evaluation processes. Where possible, you should connect them to artefacts & sign-offs so they are verifiable & misuse is discouraged.\r
\r
So practically, this is how I think you can build a robust auditing process. On a more 'ways of working' level, I think a great process can only be built in an augmented captial-a Agile or SCRUM kind of way (i.e. cycling but retaining quite comprehensive documentation so learning gets embedded, maybe you could even knowledge graph on top of this?):\r
\r
I think there needs to be a repeating cycle of research, discussion, trying, learning, & documenting in order to develop anything close to a solid audit process. I also think that if organisations/teams do this, share their approach publically, or at least across the organisation in sensitive contexts, & then discuss/reject/adopt incoming critique this process can be supercharged. On top of this, I believe that adopting the Agile/SCRUM idea of 'living' the Agile values & processes is also necessary. Accepting failure, never assigning individual blame in your team, & accepting that you will have to grow your process & knowledge by working iteratively will ultimately lead to a better process & a happier AI team! \r
\r
In short, building an acceptable audit process is quite easy to do, building a robust audit process is really hard to do; & certainly cannot be done by siloed individuals in silence!""" ;
    etd:expertId 5 ;
    etd:hasVote etd:vote-78 ;
    etd:questionId 20 .

