etd:question-20 a etd:Question ;
    rdfs:label "How do we ensure AI audits are effective and not just a checkbox exercise?" ;
    etd:authoredBy etd:expert-49 ;
    etd:belongsToTheme etd:theme-7 ;
    etd:body """AI could be dangerous because:\r
1) AI systems can have real-world impacts (e.g., biased hiring, facial recognition misuse).\r
2) Flawed audits may overlook systemic issues like discrimination, data leakage, or poor model explainability.\r
\r
What I am asking to explore is:\r
A) How to build robust auditing processes (technical and ethical).\r
B) Who should conduct audits (internal teams? external bodies? community orgs?).\r
C) How to institutionalize ethical review so it’s part of the product lifecycle, not an afterthought.\r
D) How to ensure accountability when audit findings are ignored or covered up""" ;
    etd:created_at "2025-07-23T11:44:00.292000+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    etd:expertId 49 ;
    etd:questionId 20 ;
    etd:themeId 7 ;
    etd:title "How do we ensure AI audits are effective and not just a checkbox exercise?" ;
    etd:views 45 .

etd:theme-12 a etd:Theme ;
    rdfs:label "Technology: Facial Recognition Bias" ;
    etd:authoredBy etd:expert-5 ;
    etd:created_at "2025-07-21T00:12:41+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    etd:description """**Domain**: Computer Vision/Biometrics  \r
**Description**: Joy Buolamwini (MIT) and Timnit Gebru's Gender Shades project exposed severe intersectional bias in commercial facial recognition systems, demonstrating these systems performed 32 times worse on dark-skinned women compared to light-skinned men.\r
\r
**Ethical Challenges**:\r
\r
* **Intersectional Bias**: Error rates varied dramatically: 0.8% for light-skinned men vs. up to 34.7% for dark-skinned women  \r
* **Data Representation**: Training datasets with 83.5% white individuals  \r
* **Civil Rights Impact**: Use in surveillance and law enforcement applications  \r
* **Platform Governance**: Led to policy changes at major tech companies\r
\r
**Public Datasets**:\r
\r
* **Primary**: Pilot Parliaments Benchmark (PPB)  \r
* **Source**: Algorithmic Justice League  \r
* **URL**: Contact datasets@ajlunited.org for access  \r
* **MIT Media Lab**: https://www.media.mit.edu/projects/gender-shades/faq/  \r
* **Research Paper**: https://www.media.mit.edu/publications/gender-shades-intersectional-accuracy-disparities-in-commercial-gender-classification/  \r
* **Content**: 1,270 unique individuals from 6 countries (3 African, 3 European)  \r
* **Features**: Balanced representation, 44.39% women, 47% dark-skin representation  \r
* **Labels**: Fitzpatrick Skin Type classification and gender annotations""" ;
    etd:domain_category "Social" ;
    etd:expertId 5 ;
    etd:model_category "Machine Vision" ;
    etd:name "Technology: Facial Recognition Bias" ;
    etd:problem_category "AI BIAS" ;
    etd:themeId 12 ;
    etd:views 0 .

etd:theme-14 a etd:Theme ;
    rdfs:label "Social Media: Content Moderation Bias" ;
    etd:authoredBy etd:expert-5 ;
    etd:created_at "2025-07-21T00:14:09+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    etd:description """**Domain**: Social Media/Content Moderation  \r
**Description**: Automated content moderation systems on major platforms show systematic bias in flagging and removing content, disproportionately affecting marginalized communities and non-English content.\r
\r
**Ethical Challenges**:\r
\r
* **Cultural Bias**: Western-centric training data leading to misclassification of cultural content  \r
* **Language Bias**: Lower accuracy for non-English content and code-switching  \r
* **Political Bias**: Inconsistent enforcement across political spectrum  \r
* **Context Insensitivity**: Inability to understand cultural or historical context\r
\r
**Public Datasets**:\r
\r
* **HatEval Dataset**: Multilingual detection of hate speech against immigrants and women  \r
* **URL**: https://competitions.codalab.org/competitions/19935  \r
* **OLID Dataset**: Offensive Language Identification Dataset with three-level annotation  \r
* **URL**: https://sites.google.com/site/offensevalsharedtask/  \r
* **Founta et al. Dataset**: 4-class hate speech detection dataset from Twitter  \r
* **URL**: https://github.com/ENCASEH2020/hatespeech-twitter""" ;
    etd:domain_category "Social Media" ;
    etd:expertId 5 ;
    etd:model_category "Machine Learning" ;
    etd:name "Social Media: Content Moderation Bias" ;
    etd:problem_category "AI BIAS" ;
    etd:themeId 14 ;
    etd:views 0 .

etd:theme-6 a etd:Theme ;
    rdfs:label "Criminal Justice: COMPAS Recidivism Prediction" ;
    etd:authoredBy etd:expert-5 ;
    etd:created_at "2025-07-20T08:34:34+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    etd:description """**Domain**: Legal/Criminal Justice  \r
\r
**Description**: The COMPAS (Correctional Offender Management Profiling for Alternative Sanctions) system by Northpointe is used by U.S. courts to assess defendant recidivism probability. ProPublica's 2016 investigation revealed systematic racial bias in these predictions.\r
\r
**Ethical Challenges**:\r
\r
* **Racial Bias**: Black defendants were almost twice as likely as white defendants to be incorrectly flagged as high risk of reoffending  \r
\r
* **Error Rate Disparities**: 77% more likely that Black individuals would be predicted to commit future violent crimes  \r
\r
* **Algorithmic Transparency**: Proprietary algorithm used in sentencing decisions without transparency  \r
\r
* **Historical Bias Perpetuation**: Reinforcement of existing disparities in the criminal justice system\r
\r
**Public Datasets**:\r
\r
* **Primary**: COMPAS Recidivism Dataset \\- ProPublica GitHub Repository  \r
\r
* **URL**: https://github.com/propublica/compas-analysis  \r
\r
* **Direct Data**: https://www.propublica.org/datastore/dataset/compas-recidivism-risk-score-data-and-analysis  \r
\r
* **Content**: 10,000+ individuals from Broward County, Florida (2013-2014) including criminal history, demographics, COMPAS scores, and two-year recidivism outcomes  \r
\r
* **File**: `compas-scores-two-years.csv` with complete analysis code  \r
\r
* **R Package**: Available via mlr3fairness package \\- https://mlr3fairness.mlr-org.com/reference/compas.html""" ;
    etd:domain_category "Justice" ;
    etd:expertId 5 ;
    etd:model_category "Machine Learning" ;
    etd:name "Criminal Justice: COMPAS Recidivism Prediction" ;
    etd:problem_category "AI BIAS" ;
    etd:themeId 6 ;
    etd:views 0 .

etd:theme-7 a etd:Theme ;
    rdfs:label "Healthcare: Population Health Risk Algorithms" ;
    etd:authoredBy etd:expert-5 ;
    etd:created_at "2025-07-20T08:37:35+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    etd:description """**Domain**: Healthcare/Medical AI  \r
**Description**: Obermeyer et al. (2019) study in Science revealed racial bias in commercial health risk algorithms affecting \\~200 million Americans annually, used to identify patients needing additional care programs.\r
\r
**Ethical Challenges**:\r
\r
* **Measurement Bias**: Algorithm used healthcare spending as proxy for health needs, but Black patients spent \\~$1,800 less annually than white patients with identical conditions  \r
* **Disparate Impact**: Black patients were 2.6 times less likely to receive additional care despite greater medical need  \r
* **Scale of Impact**: Affects millions of healthcare decisions  \r
* **Proxy Problem**: Seemingly neutral metrics can incorporate systemic bias\r
\r
**Public Datasets**:\r
\r
* **Primary**: MIMIC-CXR Database (PhysioNet)  \r
* **URL**: https://physionet.org/content/mimic-cxr/2.0.0/  \r
* **Content**: 377,110 chest X-ray images from Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center  \r
* **Bias Documentation**: Documented bias in medical image classifiers by race, sex, age, and insurance type  \r
* **Access**: Requires credentials but publicly available for research""" ;
    etd:domain_category "Health" ;
    etd:expertId 5 ;
    etd:model_category "Machine Learning" ;
    etd:name "Healthcare: Population Health Risk Algorithms" ;
    etd:problem_category "AI BIAS" ;
    etd:themeId 7 ;
    etd:views 0 .

etd:theme-8 a etd:Theme ;
    rdfs:label "Medical Imaging: Diagnostic Algorithm Bias" ;
    etd:authoredBy etd:expert-5 ;
    etd:created_at "2025-07-20T08:41:45+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    etd:description """**Domain**: Healthcare/Radiology  \r
**Description**: MIT studies reveal why AI models that analyze medical images can be biased, affecting diagnostic accuracy across demographic groups.\r
\r
**Ethical Challenges**:\r
\r
* **Demographic Bias**: Diagnostic algorithms show varying performance across racial and gender groups  \r
* **Training Data Imbalances**: Underrepresentation of certain demographic groups in training datasets  \r
* **Clinical Decision Impact**: Biased diagnostic tools can lead to misdiagnosis or delayed treatment  \r
* **Healthcare Equity**: Perpetuation of existing healthcare disparities\r
\r
**Public Datasets**:\r
\r
* **NIH Chest X-ray Dataset**: 112,120 frontal-view X-ray images from 30,805 unique patients  \r
* **URL**: https://www.nih.gov/news-events/news-releases/nih-clinical-center-provides-one-largest-publicly-available-chest-x-ray-datasets-scientific-community  \r
* **CheXpert Dataset**: Stanford's large dataset of chest radiographs with labels  \r
* **URL**: https://stanfordmlgroup.github.io/competitions/chexpert/  \r
* **RSNA Pneumonia Detection Challenge**: Kaggle dataset with demographic annotations  \r
* **URL**: https://www.kaggle.com/c/rsna-pneumonia-detection-challenge""" ;
    etd:domain_category "Health" ;
    etd:expertId 5 ;
    etd:model_category "Machine Vision" ;
    etd:name "Medical Imaging: Diagnostic Algorithm Bias" ;
    etd:problem_category "AI BIAS" ;
    etd:themeId 8 ;
    etd:views 0 .

